<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Belarusian People's Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Minsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Belarusian attempt at independence, proclaimed under German occupation in 1918, whose Rada still survives in exile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> Belarusian activists formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rada-of-the-belarusian-democratic-republic">Rada</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. While German troops occupied the city, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> issued a series of charters, and on 25 March 1918 the Third Constituent Charter declared the Belarusian People's Republic independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-sierada">Jan Sierada</a> became the first chairman of the Rada.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic never controlled much territory or had an army, as real power lay with the German military. Its government, led by figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raman-skirmunt">Raman Skirmunt</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-lutskevich">Anton Lutskevich</a>, organised schools in the Belarusian language and sought recognition abroad. It issued passports and sent missions to neighbouring states.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the Germans withdrew in December 1918 the Bolsheviks took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-soviet-republic-of-byelorussia">Socialist Soviet Republic of Byelorussia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rada-of-the-belarusian-democratic-republic">Rada</a> went into exile, where it has continued under successive leaders, making it the world's oldest government in exile. 25 March is marked by Belarusian opposition as Freedom Day.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belarusian-peoples-republic.jpg" alt="Belarusian People's Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: BetacommandBot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus_(1918,_1991%E2%80%931995).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Minsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan Sierada, Piotr Krecheuski, Anton Lutskevich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Belarusian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Belarusian Democratic Republic, BNR</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=belarusian-peoples-republic" aria-label="Belarusian People's Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5291 327 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belarusian People's Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5611 583L5300 622M5611 583L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5611 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Minsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=belarusian-peoples-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

